# Anonymized Triage: Building an NLP Classifier for Police Disciplinary Oversight

Jul 10, 2026 • Nicolas Cozzarin (@ncozzarin)

Share on:

### 1. Motivation

In August 2020, the killing of Valentino "Blas" Correas by police officers in Córdoba, Argentina, triggered a wave of public demand for civilian oversight of the security forces. The provincial legislature responded with Law 10.731, creating an independent body to investigate and sanction misconduct inside the Police, the Anti-Narcotics Force, and the Penitentiary Service.

That body inherited two problems that had no easy fix. First, every complaint it receives contains sensitive personal data: names, national ID numbers, addresses, and details about victims, witnesses, and officers under investigation. Redacting this by hand is slow and prone to error, and any leak undermines the trust the office was created to build. Second, the volume of complaints is large and unevenly distributed. Some categories, like poor performance, dominate the caseload. Others, like institutional violence, are rare but urgent, and getting buried under routine cases delays the response to exactly the complaints that matter most.

**Goal:** build an NLP pipeline that anonymizes incoming complaints before anyone reads them, then classifies each complaint into the office's official categories, so investigators spend their time on judgment calls instead of sorting and redacting.

I worked on this as an AI Product Owner and ML Developer during an internship with the Coordination of Information Registry and Analysis, the area inside the office responsible for this data. I scoped the requirements with the legal and coordination staff, wrote the system specification, and built the model myself.

---

### 2. Institutional context and related work

Two existing systems shaped how I approached this project.

**Prometea**, developed for the Public Prosecutor's Office of the City of Buenos Aires, uses predictive AI to read, classify, and triage judicial case files. It is the most cited precedent for AI-assisted triage in the Argentine public sector, reporting accuracy above 93% while keeping the process auditable.

**PretorIA**, used by the Constitutional Court of Colombia, applies NLP and transformer models to classify large volumes of tutela filings, the Colombian equivalent of a constitutional protection claim. It showed that transformer-based models can handle the kind of dense, informal, legally sensitive text this project also has to deal with.

Both systems confirmed that delegating first-pass triage to an NLP model is technically viable and has already been accepted in similar institutional settings in the region. Neither system, as far as I could find, publishes details on how it handles anonymization before classification, which is the part of this project I spent the most time on.

---

### 3. Objectives

**General objective:** reduce the time and error involved in receiving, protecting, and classifying complaints at the Coordination of Information Registry and Analysis, using an NLP pipeline.

**Specific objectives, on a three month build plan:**

1. Anonymize every complaint automatically before analysis, removing names, ID numbers, and addresses. Target: fully validated pipeline by the end of month 1.
2. Classify each complaint into the office's eight official categories with a per class F1 score above 70%. Target: month 2. This is the objective I am currently working on, and the results below are partial.
3. Cut the time it takes to route a new complaint to the right area, from manual triage to instant classification. Target: month 3.
4. Keep all data inside government infrastructure. No complaint text or model artifact should touch an external server.
5. Train the coordination staff to run the pipeline themselves after handover.

The eight official categories are: conflicto laboral, corrupción, inconductas fuera de servicio, mal desempeño, violencia de género, violencia familiar, violencia institucional, and otro.

---

### 4. Data

Each record is a free text complaint (RESEÑA) written by an operator, in informal Spanish, alongside two target labels: **tema**, the main category from the list above, and **subcategoria**, a finer grained label inside that category. A single complaint might read like a short paragraph describing an incident, with no fixed structure and frequent typos, abbreviations, and institutional jargon.

The class distribution is heavily skewed. Mal desempeño alone accounts for the largest share of the dataset, while categories that matter most for civilian oversight, like violencia institucional and corrupción, are comparatively rare. This is the kind of imbalance that quietly breaks a classifier: a model that learns to guess the majority class most of the time will still score well on raw accuracy while being useless for the categories the office actually cares about.

