### 12. Discussion

The loss curves in Figure 3 show the model overfitting. Training loss decreases steadily throughout, which means the model is learning the patterns in the training data, but validation loss reaches a minimum and then starts climbing again. This is the standard signature of a model that starts memorizing training examples instead of generalizing, and it is the next thing to address, most likely with earlier stopping, stronger regularization, or more training data for the rarer categories.

The confusion matrix in Figure 2 shows most of its errors concentrated between violencia familiar and violencia de género, which makes sense given how much these two categories overlap in real complaints: a lot of gender based violence in this dataset happens inside a family context, and the line between the two categories is genuinely blurry in the source text, not just in the model's predictions.

The gap between the 87% precision on mal desempeño and the lower scores on rarer categories is the class imbalance problem showing up exactly where you would expect it, even after weighting the loss function. Weighting the loss helps, but it does not manufacture data that is not there. The more direct fix is collecting more labeled examples for the categories that matter most for oversight, which are, unhelpfully, the categories that are rare precisely because they are rare in real life.

---

### 13. Legal and governance relevance

This project sits at a point that comes up constantly in debates about AI and public institutions: how do you get the efficiency benefits of automated triage without creating a new black box inside a body that exists to hold power accountable. The anonymization requirement is not a technical afterthought, it comes directly from Argentina's Personal Data Protection Law (Law 25.326), and the human in the loop requirement comes directly from the office's own mandate under Law 10.731 to be more transparent, not less, than the institutions it investigates.

The data sovereignty constraint, keeping everything on government hardware with no external API calls, is also not a default engineering choice. It is a governance decision made because the data involved is about serving police officers and vulnerable complainants, and the province was not willing to have that data processed on infrastructure it does not control. Building this pipeline meant making those trade offs concrete: choosing an open, locally hostable model over a more powerful hosted one, and building an audit log before building a dashboard.

---

### 14. Roadmap

The specification for this system includes several pieces beyond what has been built so far:

- A P1 to P5 urgency scale on top of the category classification, so the most time sensitive complaints surface first, not just the correctly categorized ones.
- A bias monitoring dashboard that flags whether the model's predictions correlate with any demographic or geographic pattern in ways that should not affect a classification.
- A defined mathematical threshold for acceptable PII leakage, so the anonymization audit has a hard number to test against instead of a qualitative pass or fail.
- Export of the trained model to ONNX for faster CPU inference on the office's deployment hardware.
- A documented REST API with OpenAPI/Swagger specs, so the office's existing intake system can call this pipeline directly instead of processing complaints through a separate interface.

